+# Real-map provider notes
+
+## Initial integration choice
+
+Use Leaflet with a configurable raster `TileLayer` and OpenStreetMap standard tiles as the no-key demo default. Show visible `© OpenStreetMap contributors` attribution on the map. Leaflet accepts a tile URL template and an attribution string; the renderer does not override the selected provider's usage policy.[2]
+
+The standard OpenStreetMap tile service is donation-funded, capacity-limited, best-effort, and has no SLA. It permits normal interactive viewing of tiles currently needed in the viewport, but does not permit offline tile downloads or bulk prefetch. The app must keep the required HTTPS tile URL, visible attribution, and normal browser cache behavior. The app's service worker must not intercept/cache third-party tiles or prefetch map areas.[1]
+
+Keep the tile URL configurable through `VITE_MAP_TILE_URL` so the owner can switch to a commercial or self-hosted provider with an appropriate SLA and explicit offline rights. Do not imply that the default public tile service provides flood-response availability or offline maps.
+
+## Application-data semantics
+
+The map can render saved shelter and rider latitude/longitude from Supabase, and the current demo store includes static coordinates around San Francisco. The existing presence RPC updates heartbeat/status but does not take GPS coordinates. The initial implementation should display only coordinates already saved in the project; it must not prompt for device geolocation or claim that markers are live GPS. Route lines between a rider's last saved position and a shelter are straight-line visual links, not road routes or ETA estimates. Demo mode must be explicitly labeled as demo data.
+
+If a real Supabase read fails, keep the last successful authenticated snapshot or show an unavailable state. Never substitute demo coordinates into an authenticated session's map.
+
+## References
+
+[1]: https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/tiles/ "OpenStreetMap Tile Usage Policy"
+[2]: https://leafletjs.com/reference.html "Leaflet API Reference"
+[3]: https://react-leaflet.js.org/docs/api-components/ "React Leaflet API Components"
